{0 Kairos}

Kairos is organized around four layers:

- a source-language frontend;
- shared semantic data and intermediate representations;
- middle-end construction passes;
- artifact generation and Why3 backend layers.

{1 Pipeline At A Glance}

{[
source file
  -> frontend
  -> common semantic data
  -> middle-end construction passes
  -> artifacts / Why3 backend
]}

{1 Where To Start}

If you are reading the codebase for the first time:

1. start with {!page-frontend};
2. continue with {!page-common};
3. continue with {!page-middleend};
4. then read {!page-artifacts}, {!page-backends}, and
   {!page-rocq_frontend_boundary}.

For temporal proof work, read {!page-temporal_phase_semantics} before
changing product construction or proof-obligation generation, then use
{!page-product_proof_pipeline_audit} as a map of the current pipeline. Use
{!page-pipeline_cost_model} before changing sharing, grouping, or backend
thresholds. The architectural boundary between the reference verification
kernel and backend optimizations is fixed in
{!page-reference_verification_architecture}. The conservative plan for
replacing edge-oriented proof emission with a grouped encoding is recorded in
{!page-grouped_product_encoding_plan}.

